(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["cam-nutrition-exam-2-animal-assisted-therapy-quiz"]={"title":"Animal-Assisted Therapy — Set 1 — PA Quizzes","category":"CAM / Nutrition","questions":[{"q":"What does the lecture mean by saying horses mirror the emotions of those around them?","choices":["Horses imitate the handler's physical movements exactly","A calm handler produces a calm horse","Horses become aggressive when handled by strangers","Horses only respond to other horses' emotional states"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The lecture gives the example directly as calm equals calm, in the context of horses mirroring the emotions of those around them. — STUDENT Animal-Assisted Therapy Dr. McInnis 2026.pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"Which quality of the equine interaction does the lecture emphasise as therapeutic in its own right?","choices":["That it is a non-judgmental and unbiased interactive experience processed in the moment","That the horse rewards only correct behaviour with food","That the session is recorded for later review with the therapist","That the client competes against other participants"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The lecture lists processing what is happening in the moment and a non-judgmental, unbiased interactive experience among the benefits, alongside an increased sense of self-awareness. — STUDENT Animal-Assisted Therapy Dr. McInnis 2026.pptx, Slide 34"},{"q":"Which indications does the lecture give for equine-assisted psychotherapy?","choices":["Cerebral palsy and neuromuscular disorders","Anxiety, post-traumatic stress disorder, antisocial disorders, grief and loss, and attention deficit/hyperactivity disorder","Corporate leadership and team-building goals","Visual and hearing impairment"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The lecture lists exactly these indications and notes studies suggest it may improve trust and secure attachment in traumatised youth. — STUDENT Animal-Assisted Therapy Dr. McInnis 2026.pptx, Slide 33"},{"q":"What does the lecture say horses seek in a handler?","choices":["A leader they can trust and who will protect them","A handler who matches their level of physical strength","A consistent feeding schedule above all else","An environment free of other horses"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The lecture states horses establish boundaries and seek a leader they can trust and will protect them. — STUDENT Animal-Assisted Therapy Dr. McInnis 2026.pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"In what year, and for which group, was human-animal interaction first used in the United States?","choices":["1867, for people with disabilities in a German community","1944, for wounded and disabled veterans working with farm animals","1919, for soldiers with post-traumatic stress disorder","1860, for patients with chronic illness"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The lecture dates the first United States use of human-animal interaction, companionship with dogs, to 1919 for soldiers with post-traumatic stress disorder. — STUDENT Animal-Assisted Therapy Dr. McInnis 2026.pptx, Slide 5"},{"q":"Under section 817.265 of the Florida Statutes, what is the penalty for falsifying emotional support animal documentation?","choices":["A second-degree misdemeanour, plus 30 hours of community service within 6 months of conviction","A civil fine with no criminal consequence","A first-degree felony with mandatory imprisonment","Permanent loss of the right to own any animal"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The lecture quotes a misdemeanour of the second degree and, within 6 months after conviction, 30 hours of community service for an organisation serving persons with disabilities. — STUDENT Animal-Assisted Therapy Dr. McInnis 2026.pptx, Slide 11"},{"q":"Which historic human uses of animals does the lecture list alongside therapy?","choices":["Entertainment, sport, fashion, and cuisine","Communication, transportation, agriculture, and military","Mining, forestry, and construction","Policing, firefighting, and search and rescue"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The lecture lists communication, transportation, agriculture and military uses. — STUDENT Animal-Assisted Therapy Dr. McInnis 2026.pptx, Slide 26"},{"q":"What was the focus of research into horses in the late 1800s?","choices":["The horse's role in agricultural productivity","The horse's ability to detect the onset of seizures","Breeding horses specifically for therapeutic temperament","The horse's ability to support physical recovery of humans"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The lecture describes late 1800s research into the horse's ability to support physical recovery of humans. — STUDENT Animal-Assisted Therapy Dr. McInnis 2026.pptx, Slide 5"},{"q":"Why does the lecture describe horses as suited to therapy work?","choices":["They are predators, so they read aggression accurately","They are solitary animals, so they bond with only one person","They are domesticated to the point of ignoring human body language","They are sentient prey animals, highly aware of surroundings, social and herd animals that mirror the emotions of those around them"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The lecture describes horses as sentient beings that perceive and feel, prey animals highly aware of their surroundings, social and herd animals that mirror emotions, establish boundaries, and seek a leader they can trust. — STUDENT Animal-Assisted Therapy Dr. McInnis 2026.pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"What is a sensory signal dog trained to do?","choices":["Alert a caregiver by barking during a medical emergency","Assist an individual with autism spectrum disorder by distracting repetitive movements with an unobtrusive signal such as a discreet nudge","Detect changes in blood pressure and posture","Provide deep pressure therapy during panic attacks"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The lecture describes sensory signal dogs, also called social signal dogs, as trained to assist individuals with autism spectrum disorder by interrupting repetitive movements such as hand flapping with a discreet signal. — STUDENT Animal-Assisted Therapy Dr. McInnis 2026.pptx, Slide 19"},{"q":"How does the American Hippotherapy Association define hippotherapy?","choices":["A recreational riding programme for people with disabilities","A mental health treatment delivered by a therapist alongside an equine specialist","A physical, occupational, or speech therapy treatment strategy that utilises equine movement","An experiential learning approach that develops life skills"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The lecture gives the American Hippotherapy Association definition as a physical, occupational or speech therapy treatment strategy utilising equine movement. — STUDENT Animal-Assisted Therapy Dr. McInnis 2026.pptx, Slide 30"},{"q":"What distinguishes a companion animal from an assistive animal?","choices":["A companion animal must be certified by a licensed professional","A companion animal has no special training in helping an individual cope with a disability","A companion animal is limited to dogs and miniature horses","A companion animal is trained to perform specific tasks on command"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The lecture defines a companion animal, also called a pet, as having no special training in helping an individual cope with a disability, used for company, amusement, psychological support and extrovert display. — STUDENT Animal-Assisted Therapy Dr. McInnis 2026.pptx, Slide 8"},{"q":"What was 'From Barnyard to Bedside', and who developed it?","choices":["A hippotherapy certification curriculum developed by the American Hippotherapy Association","A service dog training programme for medical students","A veterans' rehabilitation programme at Pawling Convalescent Hospital","An equine-facilitated learning programme developed by Dr. Allan Hamilton at the University of Arizona College of Medicine to improve nonverbal communication skills in healthcare"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The lecture describes it as one of the first programmes using equine-facilitated learning to enhance communication skills and bedside manner among healthcare professionals, since reproduced at medical schools in the United States and internationally. — STUDENT Animal-Assisted Therapy Dr. McInnis 2026.pptx, Slide 39"},{"q":"Why does the lecture say horsemanship duties themselves can be therapeutic?","choices":["Because the physical labour substitutes for prescribed exercise","Because it demonstrates the client's authority over the animal","Because feeding, watering, exercising and grooming establish routine and structure, and caring for something else builds empathy","Because it reduces the cost of the therapy programme"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The lecture states horses must be fed, watered, exercised and groomed, and that providing care is therapeutic by establishing routines and structure while the act of caring and nurturing builds empathy. — STUDENT Animal-Assisted Therapy Dr. McInnis 2026.pptx, Slide 35"},{"q":"Which unusual animal interventions does the lecture show as examples?","choices":["Dolphin swimming and falconry","Goat yoga and snake massage","Llama trekking and beekeeping","Aquarium therapy and birdwatching"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The lecture includes goat yoga and snake massage as examples. — STUDENT Animal-Assisted Therapy Dr. McInnis 2026.pptx, Slides 6 and 7"},{"q":"What began at Pawling Convalescent Hospital in New York in 1944?","choices":["The first certification examination for hippotherapy practitioners","The first documented use of horses for psychotherapy","A comprehensive rehabilitation program for wounded and disabled veterans working with farm animals and caring for dogs","A federal registry of emotional support animals"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The lecture describes exactly this programme at Pawling Convalescent Hospital in 1944. — STUDENT Animal-Assisted Therapy Dr. McInnis 2026.pptx, Slide 5"},{"q":"Which actions does the lecture attribute to a seizure response dog?","choices":["Interrupting hand flapping with a discreet nudge","Predicting seizures several hours in advance by scent","Administering rescue medication to the handler","Barking to alert a caregiver, pressing alarm buttons, staying next to the person during a seizure, and breaking their fall"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The lecture lists all four of these actions for a seizure response dog. — STUDENT Animal-Assisted Therapy Dr. McInnis 2026.pptx, Slide 20"},{"q":"What distinguishes animal-assisted therapy from simply spending time with an animal?","choices":["The animal must be a dog or miniature horse","The animal must live with the patient","The animal must be certified by a national body","The animal is used during the treatment process to aid achievement of therapeutic goals, making it goal-directed"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The lecture's definition turns on the intervention being goal-directed, with the animal used during treatment to aid achievement of therapeutic goals. — STUDENT Animal-Assisted Therapy Dr. McInnis 2026.pptx, Slide 3"},{"q":"Which benefits does the lecture attribute to equine-facilitated learning?","choices":["Improved gait symmetry and postural control","Reduced medication requirements in psychiatric illness","Improved life skills such as trust, honesty, connection, self-regulation and self-confidence, plus leadership and self-awareness","Detection of the onset of seizures"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The lecture lists these life skills, leadership building, enhanced self-awareness, and use with groups and corporate leadership for team building. — STUDENT Animal-Assisted Therapy Dr. McInnis 2026.pptx, Slide 37"},{"q":"What practice experience must a hippotherapy certification applicant have?","choices":["At least three years of practice, equal to 6,000 hours, and at least 100 hours of hippotherapy practice within the previous 3 years","At least one year of practice and 20 hours of hippotherapy","At least five years of practice and 500 hours of hippotherapy","No practice requirement, only a written examination"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The lecture lists three years or 6,000 hours in practice, at least 100 hours of hippotherapy practice within the previous 3 years, and a passed multiple-choice examination. — STUDENT Animal-Assisted Therapy Dr. McInnis 2026.pptx, Slide 32"},{"q":"Does the Americans with Disabilities Act require service animals to be professionally trained?","choices":["Yes, through an accredited service dog organisation","No. People with disabilities have the right to train the dog themselves","Yes, with annual recertification","Only for psychiatric service dogs"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The lecture states the Act does not require professional training and that people with disabilities may train the dog themselves without using a professional program. — STUDENT Animal-Assisted Therapy Dr. McInnis 2026.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"Beyond individual work, what group application does the lecture give for equine-facilitated learning?","choices":["Groups and corporate leadership for team-building experiences","Family therapy for bereavement","School physical education curricula","Competitive equestrian training"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The lecture states equine-facilitated learning may be used for groups and corporate leadership for team-building experiences. — STUDENT Animal-Assisted Therapy Dr. McInnis 2026.pptx, Slide 37"},{"q":"What must an emotional support animal letter document to secure Fair Housing Act protections?","choices":["The individual's need for the animal as part of mental health treatment","The animal's completed training hours","The animal's breed and weight for the landlord's records","The owner's diagnosis in full clinical detail"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The lecture states the letter documents the individual's need for an emotional support animal as part of mental health treatment, and is required to be eligible for Fair Housing Act protections. — STUDENT Animal-Assisted Therapy Dr. McInnis 2026.pptx, Slide 10"},{"q":"Which animal, besides dogs, must entities allow as a reasonable modification under the Americans with Disabilities Act?","choices":["Cats, if recommended by a physician","Rabbits, if housebroken","Capuchin monkeys, if professionally trained","Miniature horses, if they have been trained"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The lecture states service animals are limited to dogs, but entities must make reasonable modifications to allow individuals with disabilities to use miniature horses if they have been trained. — STUDENT Animal-Assisted Therapy Dr. McInnis 2026.pptx, Slide 15"},{"q":"Which professions may pursue hippotherapy certification?","choices":["Physician assistants and physicians","Licensed mental health counsellors only","Occupational therapists, physical therapists, and speech-language pathology professionals","Certified riding instructors and stable managers"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The lecture lists occupational therapist, physical therapist and speech-language pathology professionals, certified through the American Hippotherapy Association. — STUDENT Animal-Assisted Therapy Dr. McInnis 2026.pptx, Slide 32"},{"q":"What does the lecture say about how sensitive horses are to human presentation?","choices":["They are exquisitely sensitive to how we present ourselves and highly responsive to body language and nonverbal cues","They respond mainly to tone of voice rather than body language","They habituate quickly and stop responding to handler cues","They respond only after months of familiarity with a handler"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The lecture states horses are exquisitely sensitive to how we present ourselves and highly responsive to human body language and nonverbal cues. — STUDENT Animal-Assisted Therapy Dr. McInnis 2026.pptx, Slide 28"},{"q":"What is the primary role of an emotional support animal?","choices":["Performing trained tasks that mitigate a disability","Alerting the public that the owner has a disability","Delivering goal-directed treatment alongside a therapist","Providing emotional comfort, companionship, friendship and affection to their owner"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The lecture gives this as the primary role of an emotional support animal. — STUDENT Animal-Assisted Therapy Dr. McInnis 2026.pptx, Slide 9"},{"q":"Must a hospital allow a patient with a disability to keep a service animal in the room?","choices":["Generally yes, including anywhere in the hospital the public and patients may go","Only if the animal remains crated at all times","No, because infection control always takes precedence","Only if staff cannot provide the same service the animal provides"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The lecture states service animals must be allowed in patient rooms and anywhere else the public and patients are allowed to go, and cannot be excluded on the grounds that staff can provide the same services. — STUDENT Animal-Assisted Therapy Dr. McInnis 2026.pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"Which tasks does the lecture list for a psychiatric service dog?","choices":["Pressing alarm buttons and breaking a person's fall","Guiding a handler around obstacles and reading traffic signals","Reminding the handler to take medication, safety checks or room searches, interrupting self-injurious behaviours, and assisting disoriented individuals to safety","Signalling the handler when a doorbell or telephone rings"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The lecture lists exactly these tasks for a psychiatric service dog. — STUDENT Animal-Assisted Therapy Dr. McInnis 2026.pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"Which two questions may be asked to determine whether an animal is a service animal?","choices":["What the person's disability is, and who trained the dog","Whether the dog is a service animal required because of a disability, and what work the dog is trained to perform","Whether the dog is certified, and which organisation certified it","Whether the dog is licensed locally, and whether it is vaccinated"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The lecture gives exactly these two questions as the only ones allowed without violating the Americans with Disabilities Act. — STUDENT Animal-Assisted Therapy Dr. McInnis 2026.pptx, Slide 21"}]};
